================================== Level5 Walkthrough ============================================

--- Protections relevant to this level ---

No RELRO  → GOT (Global Offset Table) is writable
            → we can overwrite function addresses in it
No PIE    → binary addresses are fixed
No ASLR   → all addresses predictable and hardcoded

--- What the program does ---

main() calls n() and returns.

n() does:
1. fgets(buffer, 512, stdin) → reads input safely, max 512 bytes
2. printf(buffer)            → VULNERABLE → format string attack
3. exit(0)                   → exits immediately

The problem:
→ there is a hidden function o() that calls system("/bin/sh")
→ but n() calls exit() directly after printf
→ o() is never called normally
→ we cannot use a condition trick like level3/4
→ we need a completely different approach

--- Hidden function o() ---

Found via "info functions" in GDB:
0x080484a4  o

Disassembled:
   call system("/bin/sh")  ← opens shell
   call _exit(1)

Confirmed with:
   x/s 0x80485f0 → "/bin/sh" ✅

--- New concept: GOT overwrite ---

GOT = Global Offset Table
→ a table in memory that stores the REAL addresses
  of library functions like exit(), printf(), system()

When program calls exit():
→ does NOT jump directly to exit() in libc
→ first looks up exit's address in the GOT
→ then jumps to that address

Normal execution:
   program calls exit()
         ↓
   reads exit@GOT → 0xb7e50c50 (real exit in libc)
         ↓
   jumps to real exit() → program dies ❌

After our overwrite:
   program calls exit()
         ↓
   reads exit@GOT → 0x080484a4 (our o() address!)
         ↓
   jumps to o() → system("/bin/sh") → shell! 🎉

Why GOT is writable here:
   No RELRO → GOT entries are not protected → we can overwrite them ✅

